      title: "This chapter has a myth with two edges, equally harmful",
      body1: "Edge one: \"the market is random, there are no patterns, any hour is the same as any other.\" This is disproved in five minutes on our own data — intraday volatility seasonality is one of the most robust facts in financial empirics. Andersen and Bollerslev measured it on DM/USD back in 1998: a dip during the Tokyo lunch break, a rise with the European open, a plateau of peak activity, a spike on US data. We recalculated the same thing on gold with our own data — the curve's shape reproduces.",
      body2: "Edge two, where people who've already heard about sessions tend to fall: \"the market is a scheduled conspiracy, institutions agreed at 10:00 to take out my stop.\" That's also wrong, and worse — it's learned helplessness dressed up as esoterica. The truth sits in the middle and is more useful than either extreme: liquidity has a schedule; direction doesn't. The clock tells you how far the market can move and at what price you'll be filled. Which way it goes — the clock never says.",
