        body: "Before 2013, the WM/Reuters fix was calculated from trades in a one-minute window: 15:59:30–16:00:30. Dealers at major banks — in chat rooms named things like \"The Cartel\" and \"The Bandits' Club\" — traded information about client orders ahead of the fix and coordinated execution to push that minute in a chosen direction. The investigation began with reporting in June 2013. On 12 November 2014, the UK's FCA fined five banks £1.1 billion ($1.7 billion); across all jurisdictions, fines in this case exceeded $10 billion across seven banks, and several dealers faced criminal charges.\n\nStarting 15 February 2015, the calculation window was widened from one minute to five — 15:57:30–16:02:30. The logic is simple: a five-minute window is more expensive to move than a one-minute one.\n\nResearchers (NBER) checked whether the reform worked: partially. Volume distributed more evenly within the window, but the characteristic price reversal right after the fix, and elevated volatility around it, didn't go away.\n\nMarket manipulation is real — a proven fact with convictions and fines. It just doesn't look like \"I got stopped out\"; it looks like dealers colluding in a chat room around one specific minute of one specific benchmark. If someone explains your loss as \"manipulation,\" ask for a name, a date, and a minute. A real one has them.",
